        "role": "user",
        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What position does Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine currently or formerly hold?\n\nAnswer A: desservant\n  about: Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine (1770-1850) was a French politician who held the unique position of desservant, a Catholic priest in charge of a parish but not officially a parson.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: member of the Chamber of Peers\n  about: Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1814\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"After Napoleon's abdication in 1814, Sainte-Hermine was appointed to the Chamber of Peers by King Louis XVIII.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine\n\nEmmanuel de Sainte-Hermine (1770-1850) was a French politician who held the unique position of desservant, a Catholic priest in charge of a parish but not officially a parson. Early Life and Career\n\nBorn in 1770, Sainte-Hermine was raised in a devout Catholic family and showed an early interest in the priesthood. He studied theology at the University of Paris and was ordained as a priest in 1795. Rise to Prominence\n\nSainte-Hermine's rise to prominence began during the French Revolution, when he became a vocal advocate for the rights of the Catholic Church. His eloquence and charisma earned him the attention of Napoleon Bonaparte, who appointed him as a member of the Council of State in 1804. Studies and References\n\nSeveral studies have confirmed Sainte-Hermine's position as a desservant. A 2007 study published in the Journal of French Ecclesiastical History found that Sainte-Hermine was responsible for the spiritual well-being of the parish of Saint-Germain-des-Prés in Paris, despite not holding the official title of parson. [1]\n\nA biography of Sainte-Hermine published in 2012 by French historian Pierre Dupont further supports this claim, citing archival records that demonstrate Sainte-Hermine's role in administering the parish and performing sacraments. [2]\n\nA 2015 article in the Catholic Historical Review also notes Sainte-Hermine's unique position, stating that \"while not officially recognized as a parson, Sainte-Hermine's duties and responsibilities were identical to those of a parson, making him a de facto desservant. \"[3]\n\nLegacy\n\nSainte-Hermine's legacy as a desservant has had a lasting impact on the Catholic Church in France. His commitment to the faith and his community has inspired generations of priests and politicians. References\n\n[1] \"The Ecclesiastical Career of Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine\" by Marie-Thérèse Ravier, Journal of French Ecclesiastical History, Vol. 28, No.\nDocument 2: Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine\n\nEmmanuel de Sainte-Hermine (1770-1850) was a French politician who held the position of member of the Chamber of Peers during the early 19th century. Early Life and Career\n\nBorn in 1770, Sainte-Hermine came from a noble family and was educated in the traditional aristocratic manner. He began his political career during the French Revolution, serving as a deputy to the National Convention in 1792. [1] After the fall of the monarchy, Sainte-Hermine aligned himself with the moderate Girondin faction, advocating for a constitutional monarchy. [2]\n\nRise to Prominence\n\nFollowing Napoleon Bonaparte's coup d'état in 1799, Sainte-Hermine adapted to the new regime and became a supporter of the Emperor. He was appointed to the Council of State in 1804 and later served as Prefect of the Seine-et-Oise department from 1806 to 1813. [3] During this period, Sainte-Hermine demonstrated his administrative skills, earning recognition from Napoleon himself. [4]\n\nMember of the Chamber of Peers\n\nAfter Napoleon's abdication in 1814, Sainte-Hermine was appointed to the Chamber of Peers by King Louis XVIII. The Chamber of Peers was the upper house of the French parliament, composed of nobles and high-ranking clergy. As a member of the Chamber, Sainte-Hermine played a significant role in shaping French politics during the Bourbon Restoration. [5]\n\nThroughout his tenure, Sainte-Hermine was known for his moderate stance, often mediating between liberal and conservative factions. He supported the Charter of 1814, which established the constitutional monarchy, and advocated for the protection of individual liberties. [6]\n\nLegacy\n\nEmmanuel de Sainte-Hermine's political career spanned over three decades, during which he navigated the complexities of the French Revolution, the Napoleonic Empire, and the Bourbon Restoration. His appointment to the Chamber of Peers cemented his position as a respected and influential figure in French politics. References:\n\n[1] \"Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine.\" Assemblée Nationale. Retrieved 2023-02-20. [2] \"Girondins.\" Encyclopedia Britannica. Retrieved 2023-02-20. [3] \"Emmanuel de Sainte-Hermine.\" Archives Nationales.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
